  <!-- ── Income requirements ─────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Income Requirements 2026</h2>
        <p>The NLV requires proof of sufficient passive income based on Spain's IPREM reference rate. Here are the 2026 thresholds and how to meet them as a UK applicant.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__income-highlight">
        <h3>2026 NLV Income Thresholds (IPREM-based)</h3>
        <div class="msv__income-row">
          <span class="msv__income-label">Single applicant</span>
          <span class="msv__income-value">~€2,400/month</span>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__income-row">
          <span class="msv__income-label">Couple (two applicants)</span>
          <span class="msv__income-value">~€3,000–€3,100/month combined</span>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__income-row">
          <span class="msv__income-label">Each additional dependent</span>
          <span class="msv__income-value">+~€600/month per person</span>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__income-row">
          <span class="msv__income-label">Savings alternative (12 months)</span>
          <span class="msv__income-value">€28,800–€38,000 liquid</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>Spanish consulates accept UK income sources including UK State Pension (DWP letters accepted), private pension or annuity payments, rental income from UK properties, investment dividends and interest, and savings held in UK bank accounts. All financial documents must be recent (within 3 months), apostilled, and translated into Spanish.</p>

      <p>A common approach for London applicants: submit 6 months of UK bank statements showing consistent monthly pension deposits, alongside a current DWP State Pension benefit statement. This combination clearly demonstrates both income amount and regularity — two things Spanish consulates specifically look for.</p>

      <p>For more detail on income thresholds, what counts, and how to present UK documents, see our full <a href="/non-lucrative-visa-income-requirements/">NLV income requirements guide</a>.</p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Processing times ─────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Processing Times from London</h2>
        <p>What to realistically expect and what factors affect your timeline.</p>
      </div>

      <p>From the date of your BLS London appointment, allow <strong>8–14 weeks</strong> for the Spanish consulate to process your application and return a decision. London has the highest application volume of the two UK BLS centres, which can mean longer wait times than Manchester during peak periods.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Factors that affect processing time:</h3>
      <ul style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <li><strong>Time of year:</strong> May to August is peak season — processing can extend to 12–14 weeks. January to March is typically faster at 8–10 weeks.</li>
        <li><strong>Dossier completeness:</strong> Incomplete or incorrectly prepared documents cause delays as the consulate requests additional information.</li>
        <li><strong>Consulate workload:</strong> Determined by where in Spain you intend to live (Madrid, Barcelona, Valencia, etc.) — each consulate processes applications for its region.</li>
        <li><strong>Document preparation delays:</strong> FCDO apostille processing (4–8 weeks) and sworn translator availability add time before you even reach BLS.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--green">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">✓</span>
        <p><strong>Total timeline from decision to arrival in Spain:</strong> Realistically budget 4–6 months from when you start gathering documents to arriving in Spain. This includes ACRO application, apostille processing, sworn translations, BLS appointment, consulate review, and travel preparation.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Common mistakes ─────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Common Mistakes London Applicants Make</h2>
        <p>These errors cause the majority of delays and rejections for UK applicants submitting at BLS London.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__mistakes">
        <div class="msv__mistake-card">
          <h3>Wrong criminal record check</h3>
          <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Obtaining a basic DBS check instead of the ACRO Standard Disclosure (Police Certificate). DBS checks are for UK employment; ACRO is for international use and is what Spanish consulates require.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Apply specifically for the ACRO Standard Disclosure at acro.police.uk. Then apostille and translate it before submission.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__mistake-card">
          <h3>Wrong health insurance</h3>
          <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Submitting an NHS EHIC/GHIC card, a UK travel insurance policy, or a policy that does not cover Spain comprehensively without a copay clause.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Purchase a policy explicitly from a Spanish-authorised health insurer. The policy must cover the full duration of your visa, include hospitalisation, and have no copay requirement. See our <a href="/non-lucrative-visa-health-insurance/" style="color: #991b1b;">health insurance guide</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__mistake-card">
          <h3>Missing apostilles on UK documents</h3>
          <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Submitting bank statements or pension letters that have not been through FCDO apostille. Some applicants assume that documents in English are accepted as-is.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Every public document from a UK institution must be apostilled by the FCDO Legalisation Office (£75 per document) and then translated by a sworn Spanish translator.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__mistake-card">
          <h3>Bank statements too old or showing inconsistent income</h3>
          <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Submitting only one month of statements, or statements that are more than 3 months old at the time of the BLS appointment.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Submit 3–6 months of statements showing regular pension or income deposits. Time the apostille and translation process so that statements are still within date by your BLS appointment date.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__mistake-card">
          <h3>Booking BLS too early before documents are ready</h3>
          <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Securing a BLS appointment months in advance and then arriving with bank statements that are now too old or documents not yet translated.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Book the BLS appointment for 2–4 weeks after you expect your full dossier to be complete and all translations finalised. Adjust the booking if your document preparation runs late.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__mistake-card">
          <h3>Not having proof of accommodation in Spain</h3>
          <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Applying before confirming where you will live in Spain, which means no rental contract or title deeds to include in the dossier.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Secure a rental contract for your Spanish property before submitting. Many landlords will sign short-term agreements for visa purposes. Alternatively, if you are buying property, title deeds (escritura) must be ready before application.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

